"""Original replacements for memorised-response slots. Stable IDs retained.
Each entry contains a complete stimulus, answer and explicit credit points.
"""
REWRITES={}
def put(key,p,a,g,ao=None,subtype=None,bloom='Apply'):
 REWRITES[key]={'prompt':p,'canonical_answer':a,'marking_guidance':g.split('|'),'ao':ao,'subtype':subtype,'bloom':bloom}
# Practical skills: apply the skill to a defect, result or constraint rather than recite steps.
put('1-013','Describe two corrections to a volume reading taken with a tilted cylinder while the observer looks down at the water surface.','Stand the cylinder upright on a level surface and view the lower meniscus at eye level.','Upright cylinder on a level surface (1).|Eye-level lower-meniscus reading (1).')
put('1-040','State whether repeated masses of 8.21, 8.20 and 8.21 g show high or low precision.','High precision: the readings differ by only 0.01 g.','High precision from the close agreement (1).')
put('1-046','Describe the error types in these observations: stopwatch readings scatter either side of a reference; an empty balance always reads +0.25 g.','Timing scatter suggests random error; the fixed positive balance offset is systematic error.','Random error for the changing timing deviations (1).|Systematic error for the persistent balance offset (1).')
put('1-050','Name the appropriate delivery device from this inventory for a 25.0 cm³ aliquot requiring ±0.1 cm³: beaker ±5 cm³; cylinder ±1 cm³; 25.0 cm³ pipette ±0.03 cm³.','The 25.0 cm³ pipette meets the required uncertainty.','Pipette selected using the stated uncertainty constraint (1).')
put('1-057','Describe two corrections to a learner who filters sand in salt solution, keeps the paper to recover the salt, and discards the liquid.','Keep the filtrate because the salt remains dissolved; evaporate some water and cool the concentrated filtrate to obtain crystals.','Retain salt-containing filtrate rather than sand residue (1).|Concentrate then cool that filtrate to crystallise salt (1).')
put('1-059','Describe how to recover crystals from a hot concentrated salt solution when a learner proposes boiling it completely dry and rinsing the solid with a large volume of hot water.','Cool the concentrated solution to crystallise the salt; separate crystals and rinse with a small amount of cold water.','Cool rather than boil completely dry (1).|Small cold rinse limits dissolution, rather than a large hot rinse (1).')
put('1-061','Describe the corrections needed if a chromatography sample is below the solvent surface and the baseline is drawn in soluble ink.','Raise the spotted baseline above the solvent and replace the ink line with pencil.','Baseline/sample above solvent to avoid loss into the reservoir (1).|Pencil instead of soluble ink to avoid extra spots (1).')
put('1-063','Describe why a salt solution heated beneath an inverted cold surface can yield liquid water on that surface while salt stays in the heated vessel.','Water vaporises and then condenses on the cold surface; the non-volatile salt does not vaporise with it.','Vaporisation followed by condensation accounts for recovered liquid (1).|Non-volatile salt remains in the original vessel (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('1-065','Describe why a fractionating column gives better separation than a plain delivery tube for two miscible liquids with close boiling points.','Repeated condensation and vaporisation enrich the rising vapour in the more volatile liquid, improving separation.','Repeated condensation/vaporisation in the column (1).|Enrichment of rising vapour in the more volatile component (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('1-071','Describe the next two actions if a developed chromatogram has no visible spots and its solvent front is still wet. The sample may contain colourless solutes.','Mark the solvent front before it disappears, then apply a suitable locating treatment to reveal colourless components.','Mark the wet solvent front promptly (1).|Locate colourless spots with a suitable treatment (1).')
put('3-034','Describe why a classroom soap preparation is incomplete if oil is merely stirred with cold water and the mixture is labelled pure soap.','Water alone under these conditions does not carry out the required alkaline hydrolysis; even a reacted mixture needs separation and washing to remove excess alkali and other soluble material.','Alkaline hydrolysis, not simple mixing with water, is needed (1).|Separate and wash the product rather than call the mixture pure (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('7-051','Describe two improvements if a salt-crystal preparation is heated until no liquid remains and the recovered crystals are then washed with hot water.','Stop after sufficient concentration and cool to crystallise; wash separated crystals with only a small amount of cold water.','Concentrate and cool without drying out (1).|Use a small cold rinse to reduce dissolution (1).')
# Qualitative tests: supplied observations plus controls or conflicting evidence.
put('8-001','Describe two corrections to an ammonium test in which cold NaOH is added and dry red litmus is held in the alkaline liquid.','Warm carefully and hold damp red litmus in the gas above the liquid, avoiding contact with liquid or spray.','Careful warming to release gas (1).|Damp paper in gas, not alkaline liquid/spray (1).')
put('8-003','Describe how a record for a suspected Al³⁺ solution should be corrected: “white solution after a little NaOH; the precipitate disappeared in excess”.','Record an initial white precipitate, followed by a colourless solution when the solid dissolves in excess.','Initial white material is a precipitate, not a solution (1).|After dissolution record a colourless solution, not a white precipitate (1).')
put('8-005','Describe the evidence that rejects Al³⁺ in this two-candidate test: Al³⁺ and Zn²⁺ both give white precipitates with NaOH; only the Zn²⁺ precipitate dissolves in excess ammonia. X gives a white precipitate that dissolves in excess ammonia.','The excess-ammonia result matches Zn²⁺ and contradicts the stated Al³⁺ behaviour.','Zn²⁺ selected from excess-ammonia dissolution (1).|Al³⁺ rejected because its precipitate would remain (1).')
put('8-007','Describe which candidate fits X and why: supplied references say Ca²⁺ gives a white precipitate with NaOH but no or very slight precipitate with ammonia; Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate with both. X follows the first pattern on separate portions.','Ca²⁺ fits; the absence or near absence of an ammonia precipitate distinguishes it from Zn²⁺.','Ca²⁺ selected (1).|Ammonia result distinguishes the candidates (1).')
put('8-009','Describe the change of physical state in this Cu²⁺ test record: a light-blue solid appears after a little ammonia; after excess ammonia, no solid remains and the liquid is deep blue.','A light-blue precipitate forms first; it dissolves to give a deep-blue solution.','Initial precipitate correctly distinguished from liquid (1).|Dissolution gives deep-blue solution, not deep-blue precipitate (1).')
put('8-011','Describe the supported identification and one limit: reference Fe²⁺ gives green precipitate and Fe³⁺ red-brown with NaOH, both insoluble in excess. A fresh sample gives green precipitate remaining in excess.','Fe²⁺ is supported by the green colour. Insolubility alone cannot distinguish the two ions.','Fe²⁺ inferred from green colour (1).|Shared excess-reagent behaviour alone is not diagnostic (1).')
put('8-013','Describe why a learner cannot identify Fe³⁺ solely from “precipitate remains in excess ammonia” if the candidate table shows this for both Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺. State the further observation needed; reference colours are green and red-brown respectively.','The excess-reagent result is shared. Record the precipitate colour: red-brown supports Fe³⁺, green Fe²⁺.','Shared persistence is inconclusive (1).|Colour comparison using the supplied references resolves the candidates (1).')
put('8-016','Describe how to investigate a suspected lithium sample when the cleaned wire produces a yellow flame before any sample is added.','Reclean or replace the wire and check a colourless blank; repeat with the sample and look for its own flame colour.','Remove contamination and verify blank (1).|Repeat sample test only after a clean blank (1).')
put('8-022','Describe the conclusion and repeat needed if a calcium sample gives a brick-red flame but the same colour was seen with the wire before sampling.','The blank means the colour cannot yet be attributed to the sample; clean or replace the wire, check the blank, and repeat.','Contaminated blank prevents valid attribution (1).|Clean-blank repeat before identification (1).')
put('8-024','Describe which observation supports barium in a sample: the clean-wire blank is colourless, sample P gives green and sample Q gives yellow. Supplied references: barium green, sodium yellow.','P supports barium; the blank rules out colour already present on the wire, whereas Q fits sodium.','P matched to supplied barium reference (1).|Clean blank supports attribution to P, or Q distinguished as sodium (1).')
put('8-025','Describe why bubbles alone cannot confirm carbonate, and the next test on the collected gas. A separate metal–acid reaction also gives bubbles.','Effervescence does not identify the gas; pass a separate gas sample through limewater and look for milkiness.','Bubbles are non-specific, as the comparison demonstrates (1).|Limewater milkiness confirms CO2 in this context (1).')
put('8-027','Describe why a chloride test must be repeated on a fresh sample if hydrochloric acid was used before silver nitrate. Specify the replacement acid.','Hydrochloric acid adds chloride and could cause the white precipitate itself; repeat with dilute nitric acid.','Introduced chloride makes the original result ambiguous (1).|Fresh portion acidified with dilute nitric acid (1).')
put('8-029','Describe the identity supported by X and the role of a follow-up: reference chloride gives white precipitate soluble in dilute ammonia; bromide gives cream precipitate insoluble in dilute ammonia. X gives a cream precipitate that remains in dilute ammonia.','Bromide is supported by both observations; the follow-up distinguishes it from the stated chloride reference.','Bromide from matching supplied observations (1).|Insolubility contrasts with the supplied chloride follow-up (1).')
put('8-031','Describe a valid conclusion when acidified X gives a yellow precipitate with AgNO3, a reagent blank gives none, and the reference table lists chloride white, bromide cream and iodide yellow.','Iodide is supported by the colour match; the negative blank makes reagent contamination less likely.','Iodide matched to supplied reference (1).|Blank supports sample rather than reagent origin (1).')
put('8-033','Describe why ammonia after warming a sample with NaOH and aluminium cannot by itself prove nitrate, and give a comparison that addresses the ambiguity.','Ammonium already in the sample can also release ammonia. Test a fresh portion with NaOH and warming but without aluminium.','Pre-existing ammonium is an alternative explanation (1).|No-aluminium comparison tests that alternative (1).')
put('8-035','Describe why a sulfate test is invalid if sulfuric acid is added before barium nitrate, and how to repeat it.','The acid introduces sulfate; repeat on a fresh portion using dilute nitric acid before barium nitrate.','Added sulfate can cause the precipitate independently of the sample (1).|Fresh sample with nitric-acid pretreatment (1).')
put('8-040','Describe the corrections to “white carbon dioxide gas formed” when the actual observation was a colourless gas entering clear limewater and making the liquid cloudy.','The gas is colourless; the white appearance is a solid precipitate in the limewater.','Colourless gas recorded separately (1).|Cloudiness assigned to precipitate in the liquid (1).')
put('8-042','Describe which gases fit A and B using this supplied key: ammonia turns damp red litmus blue; chlorine bleaches damp litmus. A turns it blue, while B removes its colour.','A fits ammonia and B fits chlorine.','A matched to ammonia using the key (1).|B matched to chlorine using the key (1).')
put('8-044','Describe the errors in a proposed hydrogen test that puts a glowing splint into the gas-generating flask while gas is still being produced.','Use a separately collected small sample and a lighted splint under teacher supervision; do not ignite the generating apparatus.','Separate small sample avoids ignition at the generator (1).|Lighted rather than glowing splint (1).')
put('8-046','Describe why an oxygen test must be repeated if the splint was still flaming when inserted and merely continued burning.','The observation does not show relighting; blow out the flame to leave a glowing splint, then test a separate sample for renewed flame.','Continuing flame is not the specified relighting observation (1).|Repeat with a glowing, non-flaming splint (1).')
# Extraction and industrial procedures: diagnose or explain causal relationships.
put('9-021','Describe why coke can supply carbon monoxide even though its first reaction with incoming oxygen produces carbon dioxide.','CO2 subsequently reacts with hot carbon to form CO; carbon is available in the hot coke bed.','Initial carbon oxidation produces CO2 (1).|CO2 reacts further with hot carbon to give CO (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('9-023','Describe the suitable steelmaking route for each feed: plant X receives molten iron with excess carbon; plant Y receives sorted solid steel scrap and electrical power.','X suits basic oxygen steelmaking to oxidise impurities; Y suits an electric arc furnace to melt scrap.','Oxygen route for molten iron with impurity removal (1).|Electric arc route for solid scrap with electrical heating (1).')
put('9-027','Describe why a zinc-blende process that skips roasting and lets hot zinc vapour escape will not recover the intended metal efficiently.','Roasting converts sulfide to oxide for the stated reduction route; the resulting zinc vapour must be cooled and condensed.','Roast sulfide to oxide before reduction in this route (1).|Cool/condense zinc rather than lose the vapour (1).')
put('9-029','Describe the corrections to an aluminium-extraction proposal using aqueous aluminium chloride and showing Al³⁺ losing electrons at the cathode.','Use aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite; Al³⁺ gains three electrons at the cathode.','Molten oxide/cryolite electrolyte instead of aqueous salt (1).|Cathode electron gain: Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al (1).')
put('9-031','Describe the chemical roles in this supplied copper route: a sulfide is heated in air to an oxide, then oxygen is removed from the oxide to produce copper.','The first stage is an oxidising roast; the second reduces the oxide to metal.','Oxidation in the air-roasting stage (1).|Reduction identified from oxygen removal in the second stage (1).')
put('10-029','Describe why simply cooling long-chain hydrocarbon vapour does not achieve cracking, and what change is needed to obtain smaller molecules.','Cooling condenses existing molecules; cracking needs chemical bond breaking, for example hot vapour passing over a suitable hot catalyst.','Condensation does not change molecular size (1).|Hot catalytic cracking breaks molecules into smaller products (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('10-035','Describe two corrections to a fermentation trial in which glucose and yeast are boiled in an open container with a constant air supply.','Keep the mixture warm rather than boiling to preserve enzymes, and use anaerobic conditions with gas able to escape safely.','Avoid boiling; suitable warmth preserves yeast/enzyme activity (1).|Exclude continuing air supply while allowing safe gas escape (1).')
put('10-042','Describe why ethanol mixed with an oxidising reagent and ethanol merely diluted with water can give different chemical products. The first mixture is gently warmed under teacher supervision.','A suitable oxidising reagent can convert ethanol to ethanoic acid; dilution changes concentration without producing that oxidation.','Oxidation can produce ethanoic acid (1).|Dilution alone does not cause this chemical conversion (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('10-044','Describe the corrections to a proposed ethyl-ethanoate preparation that combines methanol with ethanoic acid and claims water is a reactant in ester formation.','Replace methanol with ethanol; water is formed along with the ester during acid-catalysed esterification.','Ethanol provides the ethyl group (1).|Water is a product, not the stated esterification reactant (1).')
put('10-053','Describe why an ethene polymer model cannot retain a C=C bond within every repeat while also adding continuation bonds to neighbouring repeats.','Keeping the double bond and both continuation connections would give carbon too many bonds; addition polymerisation replaces C=C with single backbone bonding.','Excess carbon valency identified in the proposed model (1).|Single-bond backbone restores carbon valency four (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('10-066','Describe why a protein solution is not evidence of complete hydrolysis, and what product observation would support that claim.','Dissolution need not break peptide bonds; evidence of amino-acid products would support hydrolysis.','Physical dissolution does not establish bond cleavage (1).|Amino-acid product evidence supports hydrolysis (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('10-072','Describe why two starch trials may differ if one is mixed with cold water and the other heated with dilute acid.','Cold water alone does not establish hydrolysis; heated dilute acid can break carbohydrate links and produce simple sugars.','Heating with dilute acid promotes hydrolysis unlike simple cold mixing (1).|Link breaking yields smaller sugar units (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('10-074','Describe why a hydrolysate spot with Rf 0.40 cannot securely be matched to a reference with Rf 0.40 measured using another solvent, and give a better comparison.','Rf depends on the separation conditions; run sample and reference together with the same solvent and paper.','Different solvent makes the numerical match inconclusive (1).|Same-run sample/reference comparison (1).')
put('11-013','Describe why adding carbonate ions can soften calcium sulfate water even though boiling it does not.','Carbonate precipitates dissolved Ca²⁺ as CaCO3; boiling alone does not remove this permanent-hardness source.','Precipitation removes dissolved calcium (1).|Treatment differs from ineffective boiling of sulfate hardness (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-015','Describe why boiling a temporarily hard water sample can leave both less dissolved hardness and a solid deposit on the vessel.','Heating produces insoluble calcium carbonate; removal of calcium from solution softens the water while the deposited solid forms scale.','Precipitation removes dissolved hardness ions (1).|The same insoluble carbonate accounts for scale (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-026','Describe why clear water leaving a sand filter may still need chlorination before supply.','Filtration removes suspended solids but does not reliably eliminate all pathogens; chlorination disinfects the water.','Clarity after filtration does not establish microbiological safety (1).|Chlorination targets remaining microorganisms (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-033','Describe why a proposed CO2 preparation using dilute acid and a metal needs correction, and how to confirm gas from a carbonate replacement.','A reactive metal may generate hydrogen, not CO2; use a carbonate and test the collected gas with limewater.','Metal–acid source is inappropriate for the proposed CO2 preparation (1).|Carbonate–acid gas confirmed by limewater milkiness (1).')
put('11-035','Describe why an air-liquefaction plant must remove water before cooling, and why ordinary filtration of warm air cannot separate oxygen from nitrogen.','Water would freeze and block cold equipment; dissolved/mixed gases are not separated by an ordinary particle filter, so liquefaction and boiling-point separation are needed.','Remove water to prevent ice blockage (1).|Particle filtration cannot replace separation by differing boiling points (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-037','Describe which component is more volatile using these data, and how that affects distillation: nitrogen boils at −196 °C and oxygen at −183 °C at the same pressure.','Nitrogen has the lower boiling point and preferentially vaporises first.','Lower boiling point assigned to nitrogen from data (1).|Earlier vaporisation linked to the boiling-point comparison (1).')
put('11-039','Describe why CO2 recovered from an industrial exhaust cannot be sent directly to a drinks plant merely because it turns limewater milky.','The test supports CO2 identity but does not prove purity; contaminants must be removed and the recovered gas checked against the intended use.','Positive identity test does not establish purity (1).|Purification and suitability checks are required (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-068','Describe the evidence for CO2 loss in this heating record: 10.0 g pure CaCO3 leaves 5.6 g CaO in an open vessel; the solid was not spilled.','The solid loses 4.4 g; CaCO3 → CaO + CO2 explains that mass as escaping carbon dioxide.','4.4 g decrease from supplied masses (1).|Balanced decomposition linked to escaping CO2 (1).')
put('11-072','Describe why salt works can concentrate seawater by evaporation but cannot recover dissolved salt by ordinary filtration alone.','Evaporation removes solvent until salt crystallises; dissolved ions pass through an ordinary filter.','Removing water increases concentration until crystallisation (1).|Dissolved salt is not retained by ordinary paper (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-074','Describe why an iron catalyst allows faster ammonia production without making the equilibrium ammonia yield larger at a fixed temperature and pressure.','It speeds both forward and reverse reactions; equilibrium is reached sooner without changing its composition.','Both reaction directions are accelerated (1).|Faster approach to unchanged equilibrium yield (1).',bloom='Understand')
put('11-077','Describe the error in a Contact-process proposal that writes SO2 + O2 → SO3 and claims the catalyst is consumed to make the extra oxygen in SO3.','Balance as 2SO2 + O2 ⇌ 2SO3; oxygen comes from O2 and the catalyst is regenerated.','Correct balanced reversible conversion (1).|O2 supplies oxygen; catalyst is not consumed as a reactant (1).')
put('12-001','Describe two corrections if a learner reads the top of a water meniscus from above and records every burette reading to the nearest whole cm³ despite 0.1 cm³ divisions.','Read the lower meniscus at eye level and record precision appropriate to the divisions, typically to 0.05 cm³ by estimation.','Eye-level lower-meniscus reading (1).|Precision based on the 0.1 cm³ divisions, not whole cm³ rounding (1).')
put('12-007','Describe two faults in a gas-collection design: the reaction flask is open and its delivery tube ends outside a gas syringe.','The flask needs a fitted bung and delivery connection; the tube must connect to a movable-plunger syringe so generated gas enters it.','Close unintended leakage at flask while preserving collection path (1).|Connect delivery tube into suitable gas syringe (1).')
put('12-011','Draw corrections to this filtration arrangement: paper lies flat across the beaker mouth, while a bare funnel containing the suspension stands beside it.','Place folded filter paper inside the funnel above a receiving beaker; show residue retained on the paper and filtrate draining into the beaker.','Paper supported inside funnel above receiver (1).|Residue and filtrate correctly located in the corrected setup (1).')
put('12-013','Describe which apparatus meets this requirement and why: deliver 25.0 cm³ with uncertainty no greater than 0.1 cm³. Available devices are a 25.0 cm³ pipette ±0.03 cm³ and a cylinder ±0.5 cm³.','The pipette meets the uncertainty requirement; the cylinder does not.','Pipette chosen (1).|0.03 is within 0.1 whereas 0.5 exceeds it (1).')
put('12-019','Describe corrections to a cooling graph whose time axis has equally spaced labels 0, 2, 8, 10 min and whose temperature axis has no unit.','Use positions proportional to elapsed time and label temperature in °C.','Repair the uneven numerical time intervals on the axis (1).|Add °C to the temperature axis (1).')
put('12-029','Describe why “the gas was white” is an incorrect conclusion when a colourless gas makes limewater milky, and what substance causes the milkiness.','The white appearance is a precipitate in the limewater, not the gas; insoluble calcium carbonate causes it.','Distinguish gas appearance from liquid suspension (1).|Calcium carbonate precipitate identified (1).',bloom='Understand')
# Forty-two marks of genuinely new data handling move from A to B.
def evidence(key,p,a,g):put(key,p,a,g,ao=['B'],subtype='INTERP',bloom='Analyse')
evidence('2-002','Describe which particle model fits a crystalline solid and why: X has widely separated moving particles; Y has closely packed particles in a repeating arrangement that vibrate at fixed sites.','Y fits because it has a regular close arrangement and fixed mean particle positions.','Select Y from the supplied arrangement (1).|Fixed positions/vibration account for a solid rather than gas (1).')
evidence('2-004','Describe the phase-change processes in two records: A loses liquid only at its surface at 25 °C; B forms vapour bubbles throughout at its constant boiling temperature.','A is evaporation; B is boiling.','A identified from surface loss below boiling point (1).|B identified from vapour formation throughout (1).')
evidence('2-008','Describe which objects are visible and what moves them in this observation: tiny smoke specks jiggle irregularly under a microscope; the surrounding gas molecules are too small to resolve.','The visible objects are smoke particles, moved by uneven impacts from invisible moving gas molecules.','Visible specks distinguished from unresolved gas molecules (1).|Uneven molecular impacts explain observed direction changes (1).')
evidence('2-010','Describe both trends in this supplied diffusion table: gas X travels 12 cm in 30 s at 20 °C and 18 cm at 40 °C; at 20 °C lighter gas Y travels 20 cm in 30 s under matched conditions.','X spreads faster at the higher temperature; the lighter Y spreads faster than X at the same temperature.','Temperature comparison uses X at both temperatures (1).|Mass comparison uses Y versus X at 20 °C (1).')
evidence('2-014','Describe which later discovery contradicts each model: A contains only a diffuse positive body with embedded electrons; B has a nucleus but assigns all its mass to protons. Supplied evidence: positive charge is concentrated; nuclei also contain neutral massive particles.','Concentrated positive charge contradicts A; neutron evidence contradicts B’s proton-only mass account.','Match concentrated charge evidence to A (1).|Match neutral nuclear particles to B (1).')
evidence('2-025','Describe the completed shell arrangement of a neutral atom with 20 protons if its first three shells contain 2, 8 and 8 electrons, and identify its period.','Two electrons remain for a fourth shell: 2,8,8,2; period 4.','Two remaining electrons complete the 20-electron total (1).|Four occupied shells give period 4 (1).')
evidence('2-032','Describe what these observations show about decay: a sealed isotope source continues to emit radiation when cooled, and its nuclei change from unstable X to more stable Y while the sample remains sealed.','The emission comes from nuclear change, not escape of whole atoms; cooling does not stop this observed decay.','Nuclear transformation explains emission in the sealed source (1).|Observed cooling does not stop the nuclear process (1).')
evidence('2-039','Describe the group and period of a neutral main-group atom with arrangement 2,8,7, using Groups I–VII.','Group VII from seven outer electrons; period 3 from three occupied shells.','Group VII from supplied outer-shell count (1).|Period 3 from occupied-shell count (1).')
evidence('2-043','Describe both trends in this supplied Period 3 sequence: Na 2,8,1; Mg 2,8,2; Al 2,8,3 are metals, whereas P 2,8,5; S 2,8,6; Cl 2,8,7 are non-metals.','Outer-electron count rises across the sequence; the listed elements change from metallic to non-metallic.','Increasing outer-electron count from the supplied arrangements (1).|Metal-to-non-metal trend from the supplied classifications (1).')
evidence('2-045','Describe one chemical similarity and one period difference predicted for atoms X 2,1 and Y 2,8,1.','Both tend to lose one outer electron and form +1 ions; X is period 2 and Y period 3.','Common +1 ion prediction from one outer electron (1).|Periods correctly inferred from occupied shells (1).')
evidence('2-051','Describe the identities supported by two flame observations using the supplied references: lithium crimson, potassium lilac, sodium yellow. Sample A is lilac and sample B crimson; clean-wire blanks show no colour.','A supports potassium; B supports lithium.','A matched to potassium (1).|B matched to lithium (1).')
evidence('2-057','Describe which sample better fits a transition metal using these supplied properties: A has density 0.9 g/cm³, melts at 65 °C and forms colourless salts; B has density 8.0 g/cm³, melts above 1000 °C and forms coloured salts.','B better fits transition-metal properties through its high density/melting point and coloured compounds.','B selected with high density or melting point evidence (1).|Coloured salts provide a second supporting feature (1).')
evidence('2-059','Describe what is wrong with a model of neon showing paired Ne2 particles that readily lose one electron, given neutral neon’s shell arrangement 2,8.','Its complete shell supports low reactivity, and ordinary neon should be shown as separate atoms rather than Ne2 molecules.','Complete supplied outer shell contradicts easy electron loss (1).|Replace paired particles by separate atoms (1).')
evidence('2-061','Describe the better lamp-filling gas and the reason from this test: a hot filament rapidly forms oxide in oxygen but shows no such reaction in argon under the same conditions.','Argon is better because it provides an atmosphere that does not oxidise the hot filament in this comparison.','Argon chosen from the contrasting test results (1).|Reduced oxidation should preserve the filament (1).')
evidence('2-073','Describe the alloy names supported by these composition records: P contains copper and zinc; Q contains iron and a controlled small percentage of carbon.','P is brass; Q is steel.','P identified from copper–zinc composition (1).|Q identified from iron–carbon composition (1).')
evidence('2-075','Describe the ions formed when atom X with arrangement 2,8,1 transfers its outer electron to atom Y with arrangement 2,8,7.','X becomes X⁺ with 2,8; Y becomes Y⁻ with 2,8,8.','X⁺ and its completed remaining shell arrangement (1).|Y⁻ and its completed outer shell arrangement (1).')
evidence('2-083','Describe the correction to a sodium-chloride lattice model that places only Na⁺ nearest neighbours around one central Na⁺.','The nearest neighbours should be oppositely charged Cl⁻ ions in a repeating three-dimensional ionic arrangement.','Opposite-charge nearest neighbours replace like-charge neighbours (1).|Repeating lattice rather than isolated molecular pairs (1).')
evidence('2-085','Describe the electron count and bond error in an H2 sketch that places two shared pairs between its two hydrogen atoms.','Hydrogen needs two shell electrons, not four; only one shared pair should join the atoms.','Two-pair sketch overfills each first shell (1).|One shared pair corrects the diagram (1).')
evidence('2-125','Describe which carbon model fits diamond and which graphite: A has each carbon joined to four neighbours in a 3D network; B has three neighbours in flat sheets.','A fits diamond and B fits graphite.','Diamond assigned using four-neighbour network (1).|Graphite assigned using three-neighbour sheets (1).')
evidence('2-129','Describe why a model can have four oxygen neighbours around each Si yet the overall formula is SiO2, if each oxygen bridges two silicon atoms.','Each of the four oxygen neighbours is shared between two Si centres, so there are two oxygens per silicon overall.','Four bridging neighbours counted with sharing between two Si (1).|4/2 gives the 1:2 overall atom ratio (1).')
evidence('2-131','Describe which sample better fits silicon dioxide: X is hard, has a high melting point and does not conduct when solid; Y is soft and melts at a low temperature. Link the choice to structure.','X better fits the strong continuous covalent network of silicon dioxide.','X chosen using hardness/high melting evidence (1).|Strong extended covalent bonds explain that evidence (1).')
# Familiar chemical explanations remain AO A, but now require causal links, not lists.
def causal(key,p,a,g):put(key,p,a,g,ao=['A'],subtype='MECH',bloom='Understand')
causal('5-001','Describe why an insulating plastic rod cannot replace a metal electrode in the external circuit of an electrolytic cell.','The rod cannot carry electrons between the external wire and reaction boundary, so it breaks the required current path.','Absence of mobile electronic charge carriers in the insulating rod (1).|Broken current path prevents the intended electrode process (1).')
causal('5-003','Describe why simply containing ions is insufficient for a solid salt to act as a conducting electrolyte.','The ions must move through the material; fixed lattice ions cannot carry current through the solid.','Charge carriers must be mobile (1).|Fixed ions explain failure of solid conduction (1).')
causal('5-005','Describe why negative chloride ions move towards the positive anode but become neutral chlorine there.','Opposite charges attract chloride to the anode; electron loss removes the ionic negative charge as chlorine forms.','Attraction to opposite electrode charge (1).|Oxidative electron loss accounts for neutral product (1).')
causal('5-007','Describe why Cu²⁺ ions are attracted to a negative cathode and lose their ionic charge when deposited.','Positive ions are attracted to the negative electrode; gaining two electrons forms neutral copper atoms.','Opposite-charge attraction (1).|Two-electron gain explains neutral copper deposition (1).')
causal('5-011','Describe why electrons appear on opposite sides of the two half-equations for molten PbBr2 electrolysis.','Lead ions gain electrons at the cathode, whereas bromide ions lose electrons at the anode; the two transfers have opposite directions.','Cathodic gain places electrons on reactant side (1).|Anodic loss places electrons on product side (1).')
causal('5-014','Describe why molten PbBr2 cannot produce hydrogen and oxygen by discharge of its constituent ions.','The specified water-free melt contains lead and bromide ions; it contains no hydrogen or oxygen source for those proposed products.','Correct constituents identified as Pb²⁺ and Br⁻ (1).|Element conservation excludes H2 and O2 from the water-free melt (1).')
causal('5-016','Describe why electrolysis of dilute sulfuric acid can consume water while the acid still provides an ion-conducting solution.','Ions allow charge transport in solution, while the overall electrode reactions decompose water into hydrogen and oxygen.','Dissolved ions provide the conduction path (1).|Net water decomposition produces H2 and O2 rather than requiring acid consumption as the gas source (1).')
causal('5-018','Describe why concentrated aqueous HCl can give hydrogen at one electrode and chlorine at the other.','Positive hydrogen ions gain electrons at the cathode; chloride ions lose electrons at the anode under the stated concentrated conditions.','Hydrogen-ion electron gain explains H2 at cathode (1).|Chloride electron loss explains Cl2 at anode (1).')
causal('5-020','Describe why concentrated aqueous NaCl can leave an increasingly alkaline solution during electrolysis with inert electrodes.','Water reduction produces H2 and leaves hydroxide ions at the cathode; sodium ions remain in solution with increasing OH⁻.','Water reduction accounts for hydrogen and OH⁻ production (1).|Dissolved sodium with accumulating hydroxide explains alkalinity (1).')
causal('5-025','Describe why a copper-electroplating bath needs dissolved copper ions and why the object must receive electrons.','The ions supply copper atoms; cathodic electron gain converts Cu²⁺ to metal on the object.','Copper ions supply the deposited material (1).|Electron gain at the object produces its metal coating (1).')
causal('6-010','Describe why breaking reactant bonds alone cannot account for an exothermic reaction.','Bond breaking requires energy; net release occurs only if forming product bonds releases more energy than was absorbed.','Breaking requires energy input (1).|Greater product-bond release accounts for net exothermic change (1).')
causal('6-018','Describe why complete combustion of a hydrocarbon increases the mass of the collected products compared with the hydrocarbon alone.','Oxygen from the surroundings is incorporated into carbon dioxide and water, so the products include that additional reactant mass.','Oxygen is an additional reactant from outside the fuel (1).|Its atoms enter CO2 and water, increasing collected product mass relative to fuel alone (1).')
causal('6-020','Describe why a nuclear power station can heat water without burning uranium in oxygen as its main energy-releasing process.','Uranium fission releases energy through nuclear changes; that energy heats the water without requiring combustion as the source.','Nuclear rather than chemical-combustion energy release (1).|Released energy transfers as heat to water (1).')
causal('6-022','Describe why a simple cell cannot sustain current through an external wire if the ionic path between its electrodes is interrupted.','Both external electron flow and internal ionic charge transport are needed; interruption prevents a complete sustained circuit.','Internal ionic transport completes the charge path (1).|Interruption prevents sustained external electron flow (1).')
causal('6-024','Describe why a wind turbine and a hydroelectric generator can produce electricity without fuel combustion at the generator.','Moving air or water transfers kinetic energy to machinery that drives a generator; the energy input is motion, not a burning fuel.','Wind or water transfers motion to the generator system (1).|Electrical energy derives from that motion rather than local combustion (1).')
causal('6-027','Describe why a hydrogen fuel cell needs a continuing supply of both hydrogen and oxygen rather than hydrogen alone.','Both reactants participate in forming water; a continuing reaction and electron transfer require both supplies.','Water formation requires both H and O reactants (1).|Sustained reaction/electron flow stops when a required reactant is unavailable (1).')
causal('6-049','Describe why a final gas volume alone cannot establish reaction rate, even when the measuring cylinder is accurately calibrated.','Rate is volume change per time; accurate final volume says nothing about how long collection took.','Rate requires an elapsed-time measurement (1).|Accurate quantity alone cannot distinguish fast and slow production (1).')
causal('6-053','Describe why blue hydrated copper(II) sulfate can return after the white anhydrous solid is cooled and water is added.','Heating removed water of crystallisation; adding water allows hydration to reverse the earlier change.','Dehydration accounts for the white solid (1).|Rehydration restores the blue hydrated form (1).')
causal('6-056','Describe why constant concentrations at dynamic equilibrium do not mean the particles have stopped reacting.','Forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates, so gains and losses balance and concentrations remain constant.','Both reaction directions continue (1).|Equal rates balance concentration changes (1).')
# Additional A-understanding conversions preserve foundational chemistry in the notes.
causal('7-033','Describe why adding a small amount of metal hydroxide can neutralise some acid without making the whole solution neutral.','Some H⁺ is consumed by the base, but excess unreacted acid can remain.','Acid–base reaction consumes a portion of acid (1).|Remaining excess acid explains non-neutral final solution (1).')
causal('7-035','Describe why equal amounts in moles of H⁺ and OH⁻ combine in aqueous neutralisation.','One proton combines with one hydroxide ion to form one water molecule; this balances both atoms and charge.','One of each ion gives the water composition (1).|Opposite single charges cancel in the 1:1 combination (1).')
causal('7-037','Describe why a salt preparation can determine reacting volumes using indicator and then repeat the reaction without indicator.','The first titration fixes the neutralising proportions; repeating these without indicator avoids contaminating the salt product with indicator.','First titration establishes reacting volumes (1).|Indicator-free repeat avoids indicator contamination (1).')
causal('7-041','Describe why calcium oxide reacting with hydrochloric acid forms calcium chloride rather than sodium chloride.','The base supplies calcium ions and the acid supplies chloride; there is no sodium source in the reactants.','Salt ions traced to the stated acid and base (1).|Element conservation excludes sodium chloride (1).')
causal('7-043','Describe why limewater becomes cloudy rather than the carbon dioxide itself turning white during the carbonate gas test.','CO2 reacts with calcium hydroxide to form insoluble calcium carbonate, whose suspended solid scatters light.','Insoluble calcium carbonate is formed in the liquid (1).|Suspended precipitate accounts for cloudiness rather than gas colour (1).')
causal('3-022','Describe why ordinary window glass suits a light-admitting pane while a ductile metal suits a bent load-bearing bracket.','Glass transmits light; a ductile metal can be shaped without brittle fracture and support the bracket load.','Transparency linked to window function (1).|Ductility/strength linked to bracket shaping/use (1).')
causal('3-029','Describe why a soap molecule needs regions with different interactions rather than one uniform region to help remove grease with water.','A hydrophobic region associates with grease while a hydrophilic region faces water, allowing grease droplets to disperse.','Grease association by hydrophobic region (1).|Water interaction by hydrophilic region supports dispersion (1).')
causal('2-077','Explain why an ionic solid resists separating into individual ions.','Separating oppositely charged ions requires overcoming their electrostatic attraction.','Work is needed against attraction between opposite charges (1).')
causal('2-087','Explain why shared electrons can hold two positive nuclei together even though the nuclei repel one another.','Each nucleus attracts the shared negative electrons; this attraction binds the atoms despite nucleus–nucleus repulsion.','Nucleus–shared-electron attraction distinguished from nucleus–nucleus repulsion (1).')
causal('2-134','Describe why a metal can stay bonded when layers of its ions slide rather than separating into individual atoms.','Delocalised electrons remain among the positive ions; electrostatic attraction is maintained as the arrangement shifts.','Delocalised electrons remain available throughout shifted layers (1).|Continuing ion–electron attraction maintains bonding (1).')
causal('5-002','Describe why electrons moving in a wire do not imply that free electrons carry current through an aqueous salt solution.','The materials have different mobile charge carriers: electrons in metal and ions in solution. The ionic solution conducts by ion motion, not the metallic mechanism.','Metal conduction linked to mobile electrons (1).|Solution conduction linked to mobile ions instead (1).')
causal('5-008','Describe why depositing one mole of copper from Cu²⁺ requires twice as many moles of electrons.','Each ion has charge 2+; two electrons neutralise that charge for every copper atom deposited.','Two negative charges balance each 2+ ion (1).|The same 2:1 ratio applies to moles of electrons and copper (1).')
causal('5-019','Describe why the balanced chloride oxidation equation requires two chloride ions for each chlorine molecule.','Cl2 contains two chlorine atoms; each starting Cl⁻ must lose one electron, giving two electrons in total.','Two atoms needed for one Cl2 molecule (1).|Two single-electron losses balance the charge (1).')
causal('6-004','Explain why an exothermic reaction can warm a surrounding water bath even when no heater is switched on.','The reaction transfers energy out of its reacting system into the surrounding water.','Reaction-to-water energy transfer explains warming without an external heater (1).')
causal('6-006','Explain why an endothermic reaction can cool its surroundings while proceeding.','It absorbs energy from the surroundings, reducing their thermal energy.','Energy drawn from surroundings accounts for cooling (1).')
causal('6-023','Describe why a generating cell and an electrolytic cell have opposite useful energy-transfer directions.','A generating cell uses a chemical reaction to drive electrical flow; electrolysis uses an external electrical input to drive chemical change.','Chemical change drives electrical output in the generating cell (1).|External electrical input drives chemical change in electrolysis (1).')
causal('6-029','Explain why two trials producing the same gas volume need not have the same average reaction rate.','They may take different times; rate compares quantity change with elapsed time.','Different elapsed times can give different rates for the same quantity (1).')
causal('7-003','Explain why an insoluble oxide that accepts protons can be called a base but not an alkali.','Proton acceptance establishes basic behaviour, while absence of water solubility prevents classification as an alkali.','Proton acceptance plus insolubility explains the classification distinction (1).')
causal('7-008','Describe why Universal Indicator colours must be compared with the supplied chart rather than reported as exact decimal pH values.','The chart relates colour to a range or approximate whole-number pH; visual matching lacks the resolution to justify arbitrary decimal precision.','Colour-to-pH comparison depends on the chart (1).|Limited colour resolution prevents unsupported decimal precision (1).')
causal('7-013','Describe why hydrogen from a magnesium–acid reaction is tested as a small separate sample rather than with a flame at the generating flask.','Hydrogen mixed with air is flammable; ignition at the generator could spread into gas there, whereas a small separated sample limits the amount ignited.','Hydrogen–air ignition hazard (1).|Separate small sample limits ignition and avoids flame at the generator (1).')
causal('7-015','Describe why an acid–alkali neutralisation can produce water while leaving a conducting solution.','H⁺ and OH⁻ combine to water; dissolved salt ions remain mobile and can carry current.','Reacting ions form water (1).|Remaining mobile salt ions explain conduction (1).')
causal('7-017','Describe why an open acid–carbonate reaction vessel can lose mass even though atoms are conserved.','Carbon dioxide leaves the weighed vessel; the escaped gas still contains the atoms missing from the measured contents.','CO2 escapes the open vessel (1).|Including escaped gas preserves the total mass/atom accounting (1).')
causal('7-021','Explain why a weak acid can remain partly un-ionised while still conducting electricity.','Partial ionisation supplies some mobile ions even though many acid molecules remain un-ionised.','Partial ionisation linked to both mobile ions and remaining molecules (1).')
causal('7-023','Explain why dilution changes the concentration of a strong acid without turning it into a weak acid.','Amount per volume decreases, but acid strength concerns extent of ionisation rather than that concentration.','Concentration change distinguished from ionisation extent (1).')
causal('10-021','Describe why restricting the oxygen supply can change methane combustion products without changing the elements originally present.','Less oxygen permits incomplete oxidation to CO or carbon instead of CO2; only the bonding and oxygen amount in products change, not element identities.','Limited oxygen explains incompletely oxidised carbon products (1).|Products still contain only elements from the reactants (1).')
causal('10-027','Describe why adding hydrogen across ethene’s double bond produces a saturated molecule.','One part of the double bond is replaced by bonding to one extra H on each carbon; the carbon–carbon bond is then single with valency four maintained.','One extra H bonds to each carbon as C=C becomes single (1).|No C=C remains, so the product is saturated (1).')
causal('10-041','Describe why an OH group in ethanol does not give the same characteristic chemistry as the OH within a carboxyl group.','In a carboxyl group the OH is connected to a carbonyl carbon; the complete functional group, not the isolated OH letters, determines the characteristic reactions.','Carbonyl connectivity distinguishes COOH from alcohol OH (1).|Whole functional-group arrangement explains different characteristic chemistry (1).')
causal('10-048','Describe why two polymers can have different properties even if both are long chains.','Their monomer-derived units and linkages may differ, changing molecular interactions and chain behaviour.','Different units/linkages despite shared chain form (1).|Those structural differences affect interactions or chain behaviour and hence properties (1).')
causal('11-058','Describe why incomplete combustion can leave both unburned fuel and carbon monoxide in an exhaust.','Some fuel fails to react at all while some carbon is only partly oxidised; these are different degrees of incomplete conversion.','Unreacted molecules explain unburned hydrocarbon (1).|Partial carbon oxidation explains CO (1).')
causal('11-061','Describe why a converter can reduce CO toxicity while still releasing carbon dioxide.','Oxidation changes CO to less acutely toxic CO2, but preserves its carbon atoms in an emitted greenhouse gas.','CO oxidation explains reduction of the CO hazard (1).|Carbon remains in emitted CO2, so greenhouse emissions are not eliminated (1).')
